            <ArticleTitle>Remifentanil-propofol versus fentanyl-propofol for monitored anesthesia care during hysteroscopy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>328-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2007.12.015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the efficacy of remifentanil-propofol with that of fentanyl-propofol for monitored anesthesia care during hysteroscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, randomized study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Operating room and postanesthesia care unit of a university hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">30 ASA physical status I and II adult patients undergoing hysteroscopic procedures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">After propofol infusion, patients received a bolus of remifentanil (group R, 0.5 microg/kg) or fentanyl (group F, 1 microg/kg) 4 minutes before starting the procedure and then received a continuous infusion of remifentanil (group R, 0.05 microg/kg per min) or bolus doses of fentanyl (group F, 0.5 microg/kg).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients in group R had lower pain scores than patients in group F (0-0 vs 0-7, P &lt; 0.05) and more stable blood pressures (74 +/- 15 vs 85 +/- 9 mmHg, P &lt; 0.05) one minute after the start of the procedure. However, no differences were observed in other variables (recovery profiles and satisfaction scores).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Remifentanil seems to be a safe and effective analgesic adjunct for monitored anesthesia care of hysteroscopic surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
